messages.content:
You get a rejection whose reason is an `AggregateError` — a single error object that bundles every individual rejection in its `errors` array, in input order:

```js
try {
  await Promise.any([fetchA(), fetchB(), fetchC()]);
} catch (e) {
  console.log(e instanceof AggregateError); // true
  for (const reason of e.errors) console.log(reason.message);
}
```

This is deliberately different from `Promise.all`, which surfaces only the *first* rejection and discards the rest. `any` fails only when the whole field has failed, and at that point the diagnostic question is 'why did *each* source fail?' — one mirror was a 404, another a timeout, a third a DNS error — so it preserves all of them. If you log just `e.message` you will see the generic 'All promises were rejected' and lose the evidence; the per-source reasons live one level down in `e.errors`. The same `AggregateError` type shows up in Python's 3.11+ world as `ExceptionGroup`'s cousin concept — modern async runtimes converged on 'a bundle of failures is itself a value worth structuring.'

