That is `Promise.allSettled`'s home turf. The summary is the product, and a partner being down is one of the facts the summary exists to report — so a failure must not abort the run the way `Promise.all` would.

```js
const names = ["acme", "globex", "initech", "umbrella", "stark"];
const outcomes = await Promise.allSettled(names.map(n => fetchPartner(n)));
const lines = names.map((n, i) => {
  const o = outcomes[i];
  return o.status === "fulfilled"
    ? `${n}: ${o.value.rows} rows`
    : `${n}: FAILED (${o.reason.message})`;
});
```

Note the indexing: outcomes come back in input order, so zipping them with the names array is safe. Two refinements worth adding in production: give each fetch its own timeout (a hung partner otherwise stalls the whole summary, since `allSettled` waits for everyone), and decide explicitly whether *all five* failing should fail the job — `allSettled` will cheerfully produce a summary of five failures, and whether that email should send is a policy question the combinator cannot answer for you.

